          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.585,
            0.046,
            0.863,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "We Distribute, Yet Things Multiply"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "numbers could be any integers. Mathematical statements that express the equality of two algebraic expressions, such as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.408,
            0.13,
            0.589,
            0.15
          ],
          "content": "a \\left( b + 8 \\right) = ab + 8a,",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.338,
            0.16,
            0.659,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": "( a + 1 )   ( b - 1 ) = a b + b - a - 1 ,   { \\mathrm { e t c . } } ,",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.184,
            0.362,
            0.204
          ],
          "content": "are called **identities.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.214,
            0.847,
            0.254
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ By how much will the product of two numbers change if one of the numbers is increased by m and the other by n?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.257,
            0.848,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": "If a and b are the initial numbers being multiplied, they become $a + m$ and $b + n$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.329,
            0.299,
            0.67,
            0.349
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\left( a + m \\right) \\left( b + n \\right) &= (a + m)b + (a + m)n \\\\&= ab + mb + an + mn.\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.354,
            0.439,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": "The increase is $a n + b m + m n$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.372,
            0.849,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "Notice that the product is the sum of the product of each term of $( a + m )$ with each term of $( b + n )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.423,
            0.721,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.18,
            0.519,
            0.585,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": "This identity can be visualised as follows —"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.217,
            0.54,
            0.743,
            0.85
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.509,
            0.858,
            0.647,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "\\left( a + m \\right) \\left( b + n \\right)",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
